      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Every keymap is located at at page boundary.\nall keymap tables in one page\n\nstructure of shift, square, and diamond tables:\n\ndc.b n                    number of character pairs in table\ndc.b inchar,outchar       translates inchar into outchar\ndc.b inchar,outchar,...   entries are ordered in ascending inchar order\n\ncapsable table:\n\ndc.b n                    number of character pairs in table\ndc.b lcase,ucase          translates lcase into ucase and vice versa\ndc.b lcase,ucase,...      entries can be unsorted, but why not sort them?\n\nstructure of deadkey table:\n\ndc.b n                    number of deadkeys in table\ndc.b keycode,offset       keycode of deadkey, offset into subtable for that key\ndc.b keycode,offset,...   offset is table address low byte\nentries are ordered in ascending keycode order\n\ndc.b char                 deadkey subtables start with extra byte - 8x8 char code for OZ window\ndc.b n                    after that they follow standard table format of num + n*(in,out)\ndc.b inchar, outchar,...\n\n*UDRL  cursor keys             ff fe fd fc\n*S     space                   20\n^MTDE  enter tab del esc       e1 e2 e3 e4\n#MIH   menu index help         e5 e6 e7\n!DSLRC <> [] ls rs cl          c8 b8 aa a9 a8\ndeadk  ^  ¨  '  `   \t\t\tae af ac ad\nBritish keymap","comment":"8  7  n  h  y  6  ^M ^D"},
        "8": {"comment":"i  u  b  g  t  5  *U \\"},
        "16": {"comment":"o  j  v  f  r  4  *D ="},
        "24": {"comment":"9  k  c  d  e  3  *R -"},
        "32": {"comment":"p  m  x  s  w  2  *L ]"},
        "40": {"comment":"0  l  z  a  q  1  *S ["},
        "48": {"comment":"'  ;  ,  #M !D ^T !L #H"},
        "56": {"comment":"£  /  .  !C #I ^E !S !R"},
        "64": {"synopsis":"Shift table of the British keymap (KeyMap_UK, used for the UK, US, IS, JP and TR country\nsettings), at offset $40 of the keymap's page, after the 64-byte key matrix. A count byte,\nthen that many pairs (key code, code with Shift held) in ascending key-code order, for the\nkeys outside A-Z that are not in the Caps table. This keymap has 23 pairs. The Caps,\nDiamond, Square and dead-key tables follow it; at reset the country setup (os/spnq1.asm)\nrecords where each starts, and the keyboard driver (os/kbd.asm) searches them."},
        "65": {"comment":"^E d4"},
        "67": {"comment":"*S d0"},
        "69": {"comment":"'  \""},
        "71": {"comment":",  <"},
        "73": {"comment":"-  _"},
        "75": {"comment":".  >"},
        "77": {"comment":"/  ?"},
        "79": {"comment":"0  )"},
        "81": {"comment":"1  !"},
        "83": {"comment":"2 @"},
        "85": {"comment":"3 #"},
        "87": {"comment":"4 $"},
        "89": {"comment":"5 %"},
        "91": {"comment":"6 ^"},
        "93": {"comment":"7 &"},
        "95": {"comment":"8 *"},
        "97": {"comment":"9 ("},
        "99": {"comment":";  :"},
        "101": {"comment":"=  +"},
        "103": {"comment":"[  {"},
        "105": {"comment":"\\  |"},
        "107": {"comment":"]  }"},
        "109": {"comment":"£  ~"},
        "111": {"synopsis":"Caps table of the British keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (lower case, upper\ncase) of keys outside A-Z that CAPS LOCK and Shift switch between. The table is empty here\n(count 0)."},
        "112": {"synopsis":"Diamond table of the British keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (key code, code with\nDiamond held) in ascending key-code order, for keys outside A-Z; Diamond with a letter gives\n$01-$1A without the table. This keymap has 11 pairs."},
        "113": {"comment":"^E    c4"},
        "115": {"comment":"*S    a0"},
        "117": {"comment":"'     `"},
        "119": {"comment":"+     00"},
        "121": {"comment":",     1b"},
        "123": {"comment":"-     1f"},
        "125": {"comment":".     1d"},
        "127": {"comment":"/     1c"},
        "129": {"comment":"=     00"},
        "131": {"comment":"[     1b"},
        "133": {"comment":"£     "},
        "135": {"synopsis":"Square table of the British keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (key code, code with\nSquare held) in ascending key-code order, for keys outside A-Z; Square with a letter gives\n$81-$9A without the table. This keymap has 9 pairs."},
