        "1721": {"synopsis":"Dead-key subtable of the Spanish keymap for the acute dead key ($AC): the character shown in\nthe OZ window while the dead key waits ($A7), then a count byte (5) and 5 pairs (key code,\naccented character) for a, e, i, o and u.","comment":"' (hires)"},
        "1723": {"comment":"a"},
        "1725": {"comment":"e é"},
        "1727": {"comment":"i"},
        "1729": {"comment":"o"},
        "1731": {"comment":"u"},
        "1733": {"synopsis":"Dead-key subtable of the Spanish keymap for the grave dead key ($AD): the character shown in\nthe OZ window while the dead key waits ($9D), then a count byte (5) and 5 pairs (key code,\naccented character) for a, e, i, o and u.","comment":"` (hires)"},
        "1735": {"comment":"a à"},
        "1737": {"comment":"e è"},
        "1739": {"comment":"i"},
        "1741": {"comment":"o"},
        "1743": {"comment":"u ù"},
        "1745": {"synopsis":"Dead-key subtable of the Spanish keymap for the circumflex dead key ($AE): the character\nshown in the OZ window while the dead key waits ($DE), then a count byte (5) and 5 pairs\n(key code, accented character) for a, e, i, o and u.","comment":"^ (hires)"},
        "1747": {"comment":"a â"},
        "1749": {"comment":"e ê"},
        "1751": {"comment":"i î"},
        "1753": {"comment":"o ô"},
        "1755": {"comment":"u û"},
        "1757": {"synopsis":"Dead-key subtable of the Spanish keymap for the diaeresis dead key ($AF): the character\nshown in the OZ window while the dead key waits ($A2), then a count byte (9) and 9 pairs\n(key code, accented character) for A, E, O, U, a, e, i, o and u.","comment":"¨ (hires)"},
        "1759": {"comment":"A Ä"},
        "1761": {"comment":"E Ë"},
        "1763": {"comment":"O Ö"},
        "1765": {"comment":"U Ü"},
        "1767": {"comment":"a ä"},
        "1769": {"comment":"e ë"},
        "1771": {"comment":"i ï"},
        "1773": {"comment":"o ö"},
        "1775": {"comment":"u ü"},
        "1777": {"comment":"make sure that next keymap is on page boundary."},
        "1792": {"synopsis":"Italian keymap","comment":"8  7  n  h  y  6  ^M ^D"},
        "1800": {"comment":"i  u  b  g  t  5  *U ù"},
        "1808": {"comment":"o  j  v  f  r  4  *D ="},
        "1816": {"comment":"9  k  c  d  e  3  *R -"},
        "1824": {"comment":"p  ;  x  s  z  2  *L i"},
        "1832": {"comment":"0  l  w  a  q  1  *S è"},
        "1840": {"comment":"o  m  ,  #M !D ^T !L #H"},
        "1848": {"comment":"à  /  .  !C #I ^E !S !R"},
        "1856": {"synopsis":"Shift table of the Italian keymap (KeyMap_IT, used for the IT country settings), at offset\n$40 of the keymap's page, after the 64-byte key matrix. A count byte, then that many pairs\n(key code, code with Shift held) in ascending key-code order, for the keys outside A-Z that\nare not in the Caps table. This keymap has 23 pairs. The Caps, Diamond, Square and dead-key\ntables follow it; at reset the country setup (os/spnq1.asm) records where each starts, and\nthe keyboard driver (os/kbd.asm) searches them."},
        "1857": {"comment":"^E d4"},
        "1859": {"comment":"*S d0"},
        "1861": {"comment":",  <"},
        "1863": {"comment":"-  _"},
        "1865": {"comment":".  >"},
        "1867": {"comment":"/  ?"},
        "1869": {"comment":"0  )"},
        "1871": {"comment":"1  !"},
        "1873": {"comment":"2 £"},
        "1875": {"comment":"3 #"},
        "1877": {"comment":"4 $"},
        "1879": {"comment":"5 %"},
        "1881": {"comment":"6 ^"},
        "1883": {"comment":"7 &"},
        "1885": {"comment":"8 *"},
        "1887": {"comment":"9 ("},
        "1889": {"comment":";  :"},
        "1891": {"comment":"=  +"},
        "1893": {"comment":"à  \""},
        "1895": {"comment":"è  é"},
        "1897": {"comment":"ù  |"},
        "1899": {"comment":"i` ~"},
        "1901": {"comment":"o` '"},
        "1903": {"synopsis":"Caps table of the Italian keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (lower case, upper\ncase) of keys outside A-Z that CAPS LOCK and Shift switch between. This keymap has 21 pairs."},
        "1904": {"comment":",  <"},
        "1906": {"comment":"-  _"},
        "1908": {"comment":".  >"},
        "1910": {"comment":"/  ?"},
        "1912": {"comment":"0  )"},
        "1914": {"comment":"1  !"},
        "1916": {"comment":"2 £"},
        "1918": {"comment":"3 #"},
        "1920": {"comment":"4 $"},
        "1922": {"comment":"5 %"},
        "1924": {"comment":"6 ^"},
        "1926": {"comment":"7 &"},
        "1928": {"comment":"8 *"},
        "1930": {"comment":"9 ("},
        "1932": {"comment":";  :"},
        "1934": {"comment":"=  +"},
        "1936": {"comment":"à  \""},
        "1938": {"comment":"è  é"},
        "1940": {"comment":"ù  |"},
        "1942": {"comment":"i` ~"},
        "1944": {"comment":"o` '"},
        "1946": {"synopsis":"Diamond table of the Italian keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (key code, code with\nDiamond held) in ascending key-code order, for keys outside A-Z; Diamond with a letter gives\n$01-$1A without the table. This keymap has 11 pairs."},
        "1947": {"comment":"esc   c4"},
        "1949": {"comment":"spc   a0"},
        "1951": {"comment":",     1b"},
        "1953": {"comment":"0     }"},
        "1955": {"comment":"9     {"},
        "1957": {"comment":"[     1b"},
        "1959": {"comment":"à     `"},
        "1961": {"comment":"è     ["},
        "1963": {"comment":"ù     \\"},
        "1965": {"comment":"i`    ]"},
        "1967": {"comment":"o`    @"},
        "1969": {"synopsis":"Square table of the Italian keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (key code, code with\nSquare held) in ascending key-code order, for keys outside A-Z; Square with a letter gives\n$81-$9A without the table. This keymap has 5 pairs."},
        "1974": {"comment":"special []+ command"},
        "1976": {"comment":"special []- command"},
        "1978": {"comment":"special []+ command"},
        "1980": {"synopsis":"Dead-key table of the Italian keymap: a count byte, then that many pairs (dead key code, low\nbyte of the address of its subtable in this page) in ascending key-code order. The table is\nempty here (count 0); $FF filler follows up to the next keymap's page."},
        "2048": {"synopsis":"Start of the LOWRAM code (rst.asm, low_int.asm, memfunc.asm, flash.asm), which the reset\ncode (os/reset.asm) copies from this bank into RAM bank $20 and runs at $0000, up to\nLowRAMcode_end. It holds the RST vectors, the interrupt entries, the OZ call dispatch and\nreturn, the fast bank binding (RST 30H, EXTCALL) and the flash chip routines. At $0000\nitself is the RST 00 reset entry: it disables interrupts and writes 0 to BL_COM, which binds\nROM bank 0 into the lower 8K of segment 0, so execution continues in bank 0's boot code.\nEntry: none."},
        "2050": {"comment":"bind b00 into low 8K of segment 0"},
        "2080": {"comment":"0020"},
        "2088": {"comment":"OZ V4.1 (and newer): EXTCALL interface"},
        "2096": {"comment":"OZ V4.1: Fast Bank switching (OS_MPB functionality with RST 30H)"},
        "2104": {"synopsis":"Maskable interrupt entry (RST 38H, interrupt mode 1): a JP to LOWRAM_INT. The jump's address\nis patched: nmi_halt points it at kernel 0's HW_INT before the machine enters coma, and\nNMIWakeup restores LOWRAM_INT.\nEntry: none.","comment":"patch vector for interrupts during coma"},
        "2107": {"comment":"A=0, Fc=0"},
        "2108": {"comment":"store A to clear byte in stack"},
        "2110": {"comment":"A=snooze/coma flag, IFF2 -> Fp"},
        "2113": {"comment":"interrupts enabled? exit Fc=0"},
        "2114": {"comment":"read back A"},
        "2117": {"comment":"Fc=0 if we were interrupted"},
        "2119": {"comment":"reload A for NMI routine"},
        "2122": {"comment":"ints were disabled? exit"},
        "2125": {"synopsis":"Default application error handler, installed by OS_Erh when HL = 0 and at process start\n(DefErrHandler + 1 when the application DOR sets AT2_Ie, ignore errors). CallErrorHandler\ncalls it through JpAHL with the error in AF. When Fz = 1 (a fatal error) it returns AF\nunchanged, so CallErrorHandler shows the error with GN_Err. Otherwise, and always from the\nsecond byte, CP A gives Fc = 0, Fz = 1, which returns the error to the program.\nEntry: A = error code; Fc = 1; Fz = 1 for a fatal error.\nExit: Fz = 1 on entry: AF unchanged; else Fc = 0, Fz = 1.\nChanges: F."},
        "2129": {"comment":"0051"},
        "2132": {"comment":"0054"},
        "2135": {"comment":"0057 (V4.1) Fast Bank binding status (OS_MGB functionality)"},
        "2138": {"synopsis":"Second half of the dispatch of a two-byte OZ call (os/oz2call.asm jumps here): pops the code\nbank into A and binds it into segment 3 (MS3BankA), pops the caller's AF and returns, which\ngoes to the function address the dispatcher pushed under them. Its last two instructions\n(noEI: POP AF, RET) are also LOWRAM_NMI's exit when interrupts stay disabled.\nEntry: stack: code bank in A of the top word, then the caller's AF, then the function\naddress.\nExit: S3 = code bank; AF = caller's AF.","comment":"restore S3"},
        "2142": {"comment":"restore AF"},
        "2144": {"comment":"address align for RST 66H, Blink NMI entry point"},
        "2150": {"synopsis":"Non-maskable interrupt entry (NMI at $0066): a JP to LOWRAM_NMI. The jump's address is\npatched like OZ_INT's: nmi_halt points it at kernel 0's HW_NMI before coma and NMIWakeup\nrestores it; the OZ update code (dc/ozupdate.asm) replaces the JP with RET while it\nreprograms the flash.\nEntry: none.","comment":"patch vector for interrupts during coma"},
        "2153": {"synopsis":"Handles a non-maskable interrupt from the Blink. If SP is in page $00xx the machine is still\nin its start-up code and it restarts at RST 00. Otherwise it saves I and the interrupt state\n(IFF2, read through LD A,I), disables interrupts, binds kernel 0 into segment 3, calls\nNMIEntry there, restores segment 3 and returns, enabling interrupts only when they were\nenabled before. It returns with RET, not RETN, because NMIEntry uses EI and DI around HALT.\nEntry: none.\nKeeps: AF, HL."},
        "2154": {"comment":"if SP in lowest page we must"},
        "2155": {"comment":"be in init code - reset"},
        "2164": {"comment":"store int status"},
        "2166": {"comment":"A=coma state, F=IFF2 in PV flag"},
        "2167": {"comment":"nested NMIs won't enable interrupts (IFF1=0 and IFF2=0)"},
        "2168": {"comment":"remember S3"},
        "2172": {"comment":"and bind in KERNEL0"},
        "2175": {"comment":"call NMI handler in KERNEL0"},
        "2178": {"comment":"restore S3"},
        "2182": {"comment":"can't use 'retn' because 'ei/di' used during HALT in NMIEntry"},
        "2183": {"comment":"IFF2 was in PV, if set : ints were disabled"},
        "2187": {"comment":"IFF1 and IFF2=1"},
        "2189": {"synopsis":"Return path of an OZ call that never calls the application's error handler: the caller gets\nthe handler's AF, BC, DE and HL. It pops the stacked S2/S3 word, clears the carry of that\ncopy so OZErrorHandler is skipped, restores segment 3 from the stacked S2/S3 word (only\nBLSC_SR3 and BL_SR3 are written), decrements ubAppCallLevel and returns to the caller of the\nOZ call.\nEntry: AF, BC, DE, HL = results; stack: S2/S3 word, then the return address.\nExit: AF, BC, DE, HL as on entry; S3 restored."},
        "2194": {"synopsis":"Return path of an OZ call that gives the caller the handler's AF, BC, DE and HL (EXX here\nand again in OZCallReturn3, so the main set is kept); falls into OZCallReturn2. Like the\nother return entries it restores segment 3 from the stacked S2/S3 word (only BLSC_SR3 and\nBL_SR3 are written), decrements ubAppCallLevel and returns to the caller of the OZ call;\nunlike OZCallReturn0, when the result has Fc = 1 and the call level reaches 0 (the\napplication's outermost call) it calls OZErrorHandler.\nEntry: AF, BC, DE, HL = results; stack: S2/S3 word, then the return address.\nExit: AF, BC, DE, HL as on entry; S3 restored.\nError: Fc = 1, A = error code; the application's error handler may run first.","comment":"called from memory.asm, stkframe.asm, ossi.asm"},
        "2195": {"synopsis":"Return path of an OZ call that gives the caller the handler's AF but its own BC, DE and HL\nfrom the alternate set (EX AF,AF' here, EXX in OZCallReturn3); falls into OZCallReturn3. The\ntail is OZCallReturn1's, error handler call included.\nEntry: AF = result; BC', DE', HL' = the caller's values; stack: S2/S3 word, then the return\naddress.\nExit: AF = result; BC, DE, HL = the caller's values; S3 restored.\nError: Fc = 1, A = error code; the application's error handler may run first.","comment":"called from error.asm, esc.asm, memory.asm, oz2call.asm, oscli.asm"},
        "2196": {"synopsis":"Return path of an OZ call that gives the caller back its own AF, BC, DE and HL from the\nalternate sets (EXX here; AF' is swapped in by the common tail); falls into OZCallReturnFP.\nThe error handler check uses the caller's own carry.\nEntry: AF', BC', DE', HL' = the caller's values; stack: S2/S3 word, then the return address.\nExit: AF, BC, DE, HL = the caller's values; S3 restored.","comment":"called from osin.asm"},
        "2197": {"synopsis":"Return path of an FPP call (fp/fpp.asm): the caller gets its own AF from the alternate set\nand the handler's BC, DE and HL. It pops the stacked S2/S3 word, sets its carry, restores\nsegment 3 from the stacked S2/S3 word (only BLSC_SR3 and BL_SR3 are written), decrements\nubAppCallLevel and returns to the caller of the OZ call; when AF' has Fc = 1 and the call\nlevel reaches 0 it calls OZErrorHandler.\nEntry: BC, DE, HL = results; AF' = AF to return; stack: S2/S3 word, then the return address.\nExit: AF = AF' on entry; BC, DE, HL as on entry; S3 restored."},
        "2199": {"synopsis":"OZ source name: OZCallReturnCommon."},
        "2204": {"comment":"decrement call level"},
        "2211": {"comment":"no error, return"},
        "2218": {"synopsis":"Calls the application's error handler for an OZ call that returned an error. Returns at once\nwhen Fc = 0. Otherwise binds kernel 0 into segment 3, calls CallErrorHandler (os/error.asm)\nwith A = the bank to bind for the handler and the error in AF', then rebinds that bank into\nsegment 3 through MS3BankA.\nEntry: A = bank of segment 3 to restore; Fc = 1 to call the handler; AF' = error result.\nExit: segment 3 = the bank in A."},
        "2228": {"comment":"restore S3"},
        "2231": {"synopsis":"Calls the routine at HL in the bank in A bound into segment 3: binds A through MS3BankA,\nswaps AF so the routine gets AF', calls it through JpHL, swaps its returned AF into AF',\nthen falls into MS3Kernel0, which binds kernel 0 back into segment 3. Used to call an\napplication's error handler and entry points (error.asm, process0.asm).\nEntry: A = bank; HL = address in segment 3; AF' = AF for the routine.\nExit: AF' = AF returned by the routine; A = KN0_BNK; segment 3 = kernel 0.\nChanges: AF, AF', and what the routine changes."},
        "2239": {"synopsis":"Binds kernel bank 0 into segment 3: loads A = KN0_BNK and falls into MS3BankA.\nEntry: none.\nExit: A = KN0_BNK; segment 3 = kernel 0.\nChanges: A."},
        "2241": {"synopsis":"Binds the bank in A into segment 3: writes it to the soft copy BLSC_SR3 and to port BL_SR3.\nEntry: A = bank.\nExit: segment 3 = bank in A.\nKeeps: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2249": {"comment":"increment call level"},
        "2253": {"comment":"caller PC"},
        "2254": {"comment":"get opByte"},
        "2257": {"comment":"get it in case of 2 bytes call"},
        "2258": {"comment":"remember S2/S3"},
        "2263": {"comment":"bind kernel0 bank into S3"},
        "2270": {"comment":"function jumper in DE"},
        "2273": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the address in HL (JP (HL)); CALL JpHL calls it. It is also the end of CallOZMain,\nwhich reaches it with HL = $FFnn, the entry for call byte nn in OS_TBL of kernel 0, bound in\nsegment 3. Called from JpAHL, ExtCallK0 and pfilter0.asm.\nEntry: HL = address.","comment":"$FFnn, nn=opByte"},
        "2276": {"comment":"caller PC"},
        "2277": {"comment":"get opByte"},
        "2280": {"comment":"remember S2/S3"},
        "2287": {"comment":"increment call level"},
        "2298": {"comment":"IY=SP"},
        "2302": {"comment":"FPP return"},
        "2307": {"comment":"call function at $C0nn, nn=opByte"},
        "2308": {"comment":"bind b02 into S3"},
        "2316": {"synopsis":"IM 1 Interrupt handler, called from 0038H.\n\nThis file is part of the Z88 operating system, OZ.     0000000000000000      ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\n000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nOZ is free software; you can redistribute it and/    0000            0000              ZZZZZ\nor modify it under the terms of the GNU General      0000            0000            ZZZZZ\nPublic License as published by the Free Software     0000            0000          ZZZZZ\nFoundation; either version 2, or (at your option)    0000            0000        ZZZZZ\nany later version. OZ is distributed in the hope     0000            0000      ZZZZZ\nthat it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;    0000            0000    ZZZZZ\nwithout even the implied warranty of MERCHANTA-       000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nBILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See        0000000000000000  ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nthe GNU General Public License for more details.\nYou should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along with OZ; see the file\nCOPYING. If not, write to:\nFree Software Foundation, Inc.\n59 Temple Place-Suite 330,\nBoston, MA 02111-1307, USA.\n\nSource code was reverse engineered from OZ 4.0 (UK) ROM and made compilable by Jorma Oksanen.\nAdditional development improvements, comments, definitions and new implementations by\n(C) Jorma Oksanen (jorma.oksanen@gmail.com), 2003\n(C) Thierry Peycru (pek@users.sf.net), 2006\n(C) Gunther Strube (gstrube@gmail.com), 2006\n\nCopyright of original (binary) implementation, V4.0:\n(C) 1987,88 by Trinity Concepts Limited, Protechnic Computers Limited & Operating Systems Limited.","comment":"IMPORTANT NOTE :"},
        "2319": {"comment":"de and ix are not preserved"},
        "2325": {"comment":"IFF1 and IFF2 = 1"},
        "2326": {"comment":"RETI is not necessary since there is no Z80 PIO"},
        "2327": {"synopsis":"RET is faster (10T vs 14T)\nNote : RETI=RETN=RET + copy IFF2 to IFF1\nTimings to enter and exit LOW_INT : 120 T-states"},
        "2331": {"comment":"priority to serial RX interrupt"},
        "2334": {"comment":"serial idle counter"},
        "2338": {"comment":"zero means serial RX interrupt inactive"},
        "2341": {"comment":"else decrement, store and ret"},
        "2345": {"comment":"soft copy of INT"},
        "2348": {"comment":"current interrupt status (STA)"},
        "2350": {"comment":"mask"},
        "2351": {"comment":"test BM_INTGINT & BM_STATIME"},
        "2352": {"comment":"STA.TIME = 0, not a TIME int, skip ..."},
        "2355": {"comment":"hl = BLSC_INT"},
        "2357": {"comment":"INT.TIME (RTC) disabled? skip"},
        "2360": {"comment":"point softcopy to RTC enable"},
        "2362": {"comment":"RTC int status"},
        "2364": {"comment":"mask out disabled ones"},
        "2367": {"comment":"not a TICK? skip"},
        "2372": {"comment":"ack TICK"},
        "2389": {"comment":"timer=-1, skip"},
        "2393": {"comment":"timer=0, skip"},
        "2403": {"comment":"if timer=0 set pending SMT"},
        "2407": {"comment":"reload hl (was corrupted by IntBeeper)"},
        "2415": {"comment":"set during switch off (nmi.asm)"},
        "2417": {"comment":"scan active? exit int"},
        "2422": {"comment":"read all columns"},
        "2426": {"comment":"cancel current"},
        "2430": {"comment":"and prev"},
        "2431": {"comment":"check if any key pressed (all)"},
        "2434": {"comment":"no keys? exit"},
        "2438": {"comment":"no shifts? exit"},
        "2442": {"comment":"remove flag before switching off"},
        "2444": {"comment":"exit interrupt"},
        "2445": {"synopsis":"Calls kernel 0's BothShifts through ExtCallK0 (on the system stack, with kernel 0 bound into\nsegment 3) and returns its result. Used by the interrupt handler while the keyboard is\nlocked, to see whether both Shift keys are down.\nEntry: none.\nExit: Fc = 0 when only both Shift keys (and maybe Shift Lock) are down, A bit 4 = Shift\nLock; Fc = 1, Fz = 1 when one Shift key is down; Fc = 1, Fz = 0 when other keys are down.\nChanges: AF, BC.\nKeeps: DE, HL."},
        "2448": {"comment":"and RET"},
        "2450": {"synopsis":"keyboard scan","comment":"read all columns"},
        "2454": {"comment":"key pressed? reset timeout"},
        "2457": {"comment":"key pressed? force keyboard scan"},
        "2459": {"comment":"check if we have active keys"},
        "2465": {"comment":"current"},
        "2469": {"comment":"prev"},
        "2470": {"comment":"no key"},
        "2474": {"comment":"and RET"},
        "2476": {"synopsis":"no ints outside blink or RTC disabled","comment":"ignore STA[2] (always 0)"},
        "2478": {"comment":"STA.KEY = 0 (no KB interrupt), skip"},
        "2482": {"comment":"ack keyboard interrupt and exit (wake-up from snooze or coma)"},
        "2486": {"comment":"STA.BTL (Battery low) = 0, skip"},
        "2488": {"comment":"disable int"},
        "2491": {"comment":"really"},
        "2495": {"comment":"ack BATLOW"},
        "2502": {"comment":"request OZ window update"},
        "2507": {"comment":"STA.UART = 0, skip"},
        "2509": {"comment":"dont process TDRE, CTS or DCD interrupt"},
        "2510": {"comment":"latency to process RX buffer before processing all kind of interrupt"},
        "2512": {"comment":"reset serial idle counter every real RDRF interrupt"},
        "2515": {"comment":"h = ubRXBRdPos"},
        "2518": {"comment":"c = ubRXBRdPos"},
        "2519": {"comment":"l = ubRXBWrPos"},
        "2523": {"comment":"buffer full, disable UART interrupt"},
        "2532": {"comment":"read serial data and acknowledge UIT.RDRF"},
        "2534": {"comment":"if XONXOFF or parity bit required"},
        "2536": {"synopsis":"(13+4+7+11+7=42)","comment":"RX Buffer Page"},
        "2538": {"comment":"put byte in buffer"},
        "2541": {"comment":"update WrPos"},
        "2544": {"comment":"used = Wr-Rd"},
        "2549": {"comment":"block sender if more than RXB_LIM bytes filled"},
        "2552": {"synopsis":"(7+7+4+4+13+4+7+11=57)"},
        "2555": {"comment":"already low ?"},
        "2562": {"comment":"reset RTS line"},
        "2567": {"comment":"no flow control? exit"},
        "2576": {"comment":"send XOFF and RET to LOWRAM_INT"},
        "2578": {"comment":"clear parity bit, check for XON/XOFF if needed"},
        "2580": {"comment":"no parity? 8-bit data"},
        "2582": {"comment":"else clear parity bit"},
        "2586": {"comment":"no flow control? get char"},
        "2590": {"comment":"send XON"},
        "2594": {"comment":"get the byte"},
        "2599": {"comment":"stop sending"},
        "2605": {"comment":"allow sending"},
        "2608": {"synopsis":"Disables the UART interrupt (clears BM_INTUART in BLSC_INT and writes it to BL_INT) until\nthe serial receive buffer is emptied. Called by the RDRF interrupt code when the buffer is\nfull.\nEntry: none.\nChanges: AF."},
        "2611": {"comment":"disable UART interrupt"},
        "2613": {"comment":"until buffer is emptied"},
        "2622": {"comment":"handle STA.FLAP interrupt"},
        "2625": {"comment":"and RET to LOWRAM_INT"},
        "2629": {"comment":"handle STA.A19 interrupt"},
        "2631": {"comment":"(w) main int. mask"},
        "2634": {"synopsis":"Reloads the automatic switch-off counter ubTimeoutCnt from the Panel's time-out ubTimeout\n(minutes). A non-zero value is stored plus one, so the counter is not 30 seconds shorter\nthan set; 0 (no time-out) is stored as it is. Called on each key press and while the small\ntimer runs.\nEntry: none.\nKeeps: all registers."},
        "2635": {"comment":"get the current Timeout (in minutes) parameter stored by Panel"},
        "2639": {"comment":"zero means no time out, store this value too"},
        "2641": {"comment":"if not 30 sec less than expected"},
        "2649": {"comment":"no SEC? skip"},
        "2651": {"comment":"acknowledge RTC SEC interrupt"},
        "2654": {"comment":"and RET to LOWRAM_INT"},
        "2659": {"comment":"acknowledge RTC MIN interrupt"},
        "2662": {"comment":"and RET to LOWRAM_INT"},
        "2664": {"synopsis":"Drives the beeper on each TICK interrupt (unless ITSK_B_PREEMPTION is set).\n1. With no sound running (ubSoundCount = 0): a pending keyclick (KBF_B_BEEP) is cleared and\nturns the speaker on; otherwise the speaker is silenced if it runs.\n2. Otherwise it sets the speaker for the current phase (intb_beep4: odd ubSoundCount = mark,\nsounding; even = space) and decrements ubSoundActive.\n3. When that reaches 0 it decrements ubSoundCount and loads ubSoundActive with the next\nspace or mark count, skipping zero counts; at count 0 the speaker is silenced. The speaker\nis set by bit BB_COMSRUN of BLSC_COM, written to BL_COM.\nEntry: none.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"beep active"},
        "2674": {"comment":"keyclick pending?"},
        "2678": {"comment":"silence beeper if not already"},
        "2690": {"comment":"do keyclick"},
        "2699": {"comment":"ubSoundActive"},
        "2709": {"comment":"even=space, odd=mark"},
        "2711": {"comment":"space count"},
        "2714": {"comment":"mark count"},
        "2722": {"comment":"zero count? ignore it"},
        "2724": {"synopsis":"Sets the speaker for a beep phase: clears BB_COMSRUN in BLSC_COM, sets it again when Fc = 1\nand the sound setting cSound is 'Y' (the 3K2 tone drives the speaker), and writes BLSC_COM\nto BL_COM.\nEntry: Fc = 1 for a mark (sounding) phase, Fc = 0 for a space.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "2727": {"comment":"speaker source SBIT"},
        "2741": {"comment":"speaker source 3K2"},
        "2744": {"comment":"(w) command register"},
        "2747": {"synopsis":"Special fast extended call for Kernel0\nSP, DE, AF' are preserved\nIX,IY,BC',DE',HL' have to preserved in the call","comment":"push hl, get PC"},
        "2748": {"comment":"save de if subcall destroys it too"},
        "2749": {"comment":"de = (SP)"},
        "2750": {"comment":"stack above $2000? use system stack"},
        "2753": {"comment":"bad application like basic moves stack"},
        "2759": {"comment":"read new SP"},
        "2765": {"comment":"preserve af'"},
        "2766": {"comment":"remember S2"},
        "2773": {"comment":"function = (SP)"},
        "2774": {"comment":"get function in HL"},
        "2778": {"comment":"and call it"},
        "2781": {"comment":"preserve af in af' (BothShift returns Fz and Fc)"},
        "2782": {"comment":"restore S2"},
        "2786": {"comment":"restore af'"},
        "2788": {"comment":"stack"},
        "2789": {"comment":"restore stack"},
        "2793": {"synopsis":"LOWRAM fast memory bank switching and extended call routines.\n\nThis file is part of the Z88 operating system, OZ.     0000000000000000      ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\n000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nOZ is free software; you can redistribute it and/    0000            0000              ZZZZZ\nor modify it under the terms of the GNU General      0000            0000            ZZZZZ\nPublic License as published by the Free Software     0000            0000          ZZZZZ\nFoundation; either version 2, or (at your option)    0000            0000        ZZZZZ\nany later version. OZ is distributed in the hope     0000            0000      ZZZZZ\nthat it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;    0000            0000    ZZZZZ\nwithout even the implied warranty of MERCHANTA-       000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nBILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See        0000000000000000  ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nthe GNU General Public License for more details.\nYou should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along with OZ; see the file\nCOPYING. If not, write to:\nFree Software Foundation, Inc.\n59 Temple Place-Suite 330,\nBoston, MA 02111-1307, USA.\n\nSource code was reverse engineered from OZ 4.0 (UK) ROM and made compilable by Jorma Oksanen.\nAdditional development improvements, comments, definitions and new implementations by\n(C) Jorma Oksanen (jorma.oksanen@gmail.com), 2003\n(C) Thierry Peycru (pek@users.sf.net), 2005\n(C) Gunther Strube (gstrube@gmail.com), 2005\n\nCopyright of original (binary) implementation, V4.0:\n(C) 1987,88 by Trinity Concepts Limited, Protechnic Computers Limited & Operating Systems Limited.\n\nBind bank, defined in B, into segment C (MS_Sx). Return old bank binding in B.\nThis is the functional equivalent of original OS_MPB, but much faster.\n\nRegister affected on return:\nAF.CDEHL/IXIY same\n..B...../.... different"},
        "2795": {"comment":"base of SR0 - SR3 soft copies"},
        "2798": {"comment":"get segment specifier (MS_Sx)"},
        "2799": {"comment":"preserve only segment specifier range..."},
        "2802": {"comment":"HL points at Blink soft copy of current binding in segment C"},
        "2803": {"comment":"get bound bank number in current segment"},
        "2804": {"comment":"A contains \"old\" bank number"},
        "2805": {"comment":"preserve original MS_Sx"},
        "2807": {"comment":"bind..."},
        "2809": {"comment":"return previous bank binding"},
        "2810": {"comment":"restore C"},
        "2814": {"synopsis":"Get current Bank binding for specified segment MS_Sx, defined in C.\nThis is the functional equivalent of OS_MGB, but much faster.\n\nRegister affected on return:\nAF.CDEHL/IXIY same\n..B...../.... different"},
        "2816": {"comment":"base of SR0 - SR3 soft copies"},
        "2819": {"comment":"get segment specifier (MS_Sx)"},
        "2820": {"comment":"preserve only segment specifier..."},
        "2823": {"comment":"HL points at Blink soft copy of current binding in segment C"},
        "2824": {"comment":"get current bank binding"},
        "2828": {"synopsis":"EXTCALL - 24/32bit Call Subroutine in external bank\n\nThis routine is executed by the RST 28H vector. Both RST 28H and this routine is located\nin LOWRAM. The 32bit address is available in low byte - high byte order following the RST 28H\ninstruction opcode. The segment specifier (bits 15,14) of the lower 16bit address automatically\nidentifies where the bank will be bound into the Z80 address space. The bank number of the 32bit\naddress may be specified as absolute or as slot relative ($00 - $3F).\n\nThe ExtCall does not destroy any register call arguments or return values.\nExtCall is to be regarded as a normal CALL instruction, but for 24/32bit address range.\n\nThe Extcall uses 32bit storage notation for future expansion.\nOn the Z88, only 24bit addressing is available (8bit bank and 16bit local address space).\nFuture Z80 hardware will allow 16bit bank addressing.\n\nExample in Z80 assembler, macro notation: EXTCALL $C000,$FE (a RST 28H followed by 24bit address)\nExecute code in bank $FE at address $C000 bound into segment 3 (the instruction opcode sequence\nin memory is $EF, $00, $C0, $FE, $00). This call instruction uses an absolute bank (located in slot 3)\nin the 24bit address, ie. a piece of code in slot 2 might want to execute a subroutine in slot 3.\n\nSlot relative subroutine calls defines the bank number in the range $00 - $3F (same principle\nas for DOR pointers in application cards). A instantiated large application using more than\n32K code space (that typically needs the remaining free segments in the Z80 address space for\ndynamic data structure management) would use the 24bit slot relative call to execute subroutines\nin banks that are not bound in the Z80 address by default as defined by the Application DOR).","comment":"this space will be updated with current bank bindings"},
        "2829": {"comment":"this space will be updated with [restore_bank_binding] routine address"},
        "2840": {"comment":"IY points at base of register push frame"},
        "2845": {"comment":"HL points at low byte of 24bit address argument of EXTCALL"},
        "2848": {"comment":"get low byte of call address"},
        "2850": {"comment":"get high byte of call address"},
        "2851": {"comment":"point to bank number of 24bit address"},
        "2852": {"comment":"get bank number of call address"},
        "2855": {"comment":"if bank number is slot relative, then get slot mask of executing code"},
        "2858": {"comment":"and merge with relative bank number"},
        "2859": {"comment":"skip 4th byte of 32bit address (currently unused)"},
        "2860": {"comment":"point at opcode of next instruction"},
        "2864": {"comment":"updated RETurn address on stack (first instruction opcode after EXTCALL 32 bit address argument)"},
        "2867": {"comment":"HL = address of sub-routine to execute in bank..."},
        "2868": {"comment":"the bank number to bind into segment of CALL address"},
        "2869": {"comment":"bits 15,14 of Z80 CALL address in HL contains segment mask..."},
        "2872": {"comment":"prepare segment specifier in bits 0,1 (remaining bits are stripped by RST 30H)"},
        "2874": {"comment":"bind in bank of 24bit address (returned B = old bank binding)"},
        "2881": {"comment":"call'ed subroutine RETurns to restore the old bank binding before"},
        "2885": {"comment":"returning to instruction after EXTCALL instruction"},
        "2888": {"comment":"preserve old bank binding of destination CALL segment"},
        "2896": {"comment":"restored original AF, BC, DE, HL & IY registers"},
        "2897": {"comment":"execute subroutine in bound bank"},
        "2898": {"synopsis":"OZ source name: restore_bnk_binding."},
        "2901": {"comment":"old bank binding..."},
        "2902": {"comment":"restore previous bank binding"},
        "2906": {"comment":"restored original returned BC, HL registers from EXTCALL'ed sub-routine."},
        "2907": {"comment":"execute instruction that follows the EXTCALL instruction"},
        "2908": {"synopsis":"Finds the slot of the code that made an EXTCALL with a slot-relative bank ($00-$3F): reads\nthe soft copy of the bank bound in the segment that H addresses and returns its slot bits.\nExtCall ORs them with the relative bank number.\nOZ source name: get_slotrelative_bank.\nEntry: H = high byte of an address in the calling code.\nExit: A = slot mask (bits 7-6 of the bound bank); BC = address of the segment's soft copy.\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"base of SR0 - SR3 soft copies"},
        "2911": {"comment":"bits 15,14 of executing code define the segment mask"},
        "2915": {"comment":"convert to segment specifier (0-3), then"},
        "2916": {"comment":"get Blink soft copy bank bindings from address $04D0 - $04D3"},
        "2917": {"comment":"BC points at Blink soft copy of current binding in segment C"},
        "2918": {"comment":"get current bank binding of executing code"},
        "2919": {"comment":"and return only the slot mask"},
        "2922": {"synopsis":"Lowram routines for OS_Fep API.\n\nThis file is part of the Z88 operating system, OZ.     0000000000000000      ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\n000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nOZ is free software; you can redistribute it and/    0000            0000              ZZZZZ\nor modify it under the terms of the GNU General      0000            0000            ZZZZZ\nPublic License as published by the Free Software     0000            0000          ZZZZZ\nFoundation; either version 2, or (at your option)    0000            0000        ZZZZZ\nany later version. OZ is distributed in the hope     0000            0000      ZZZZZ\nthat it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;    0000            0000    ZZZZZ\nwithout even the implied warranty of MERCHANTA-       000000000000000000   ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nBILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See        0000000000000000  ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ\nthe GNU General Public License for more details.\nYou should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along with OZ; see the file\nCOPYING. If not, write to:\nFree Software Foundation, Inc.\n59 Temple Place-Suite 330,\nBoston, MA 02111-1307, USA.\n\n(C) Thierry Peycru (pek@users.sf.net), 2005\n(C) Gunther Strube (gstrube@gmail.com), 2005\n\nPoll for I28F0xxxx (INTEL) Flash Memory Chip ID.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nEntry: HL points into bound bank of potential Flash Memory.\nExit: H = manufacturer code (at $00 0000 on chip); L = device code (at $00 0001 on chip).\nChanges: DE, HL.\nKeeps: AF, BC, IX, IY.","comment":"get INTELligent identification code (manufacturer and device)"},
        "2924": {"comment":"D = Manufacturer Code (at $00 0000)"},
        "2926": {"comment":"E = Device Code (at $00 0001)"},
        "2927": {"comment":"Reset Flash Memory Chip to read array mode"},
        "2931": {"synopsis":"Erase sector in I28F0xxxx (INTEL) Flash Memory Chip, identified by executing chip command\nin bank of sector to be erased.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nOZ source name: I28Fx_EraseSector.\nEntry: HL points into bound bank of Flash Memory sector to be erased.\nExit: A = Chip Status Register; (bit 3 enabled indicates missing VPP pin during erase); (bit\n5 enabled indicates erase sector failure).\nChanges: AF.\nKeeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.","comment":"erase sector command"},
        "2933": {"comment":"confirm erasure"},
        "2938": {"synopsis":"OZ source name: I28Fx_ReadArrayMode.","comment":"Clear Status Register"},
        "2940": {"comment":"Reset Flash Memory to Read Array Mode"},
        "2943": {"synopsis":"Blow byte in I28F0xxxx (INTEL) Flash Memory Chip, identified by executing chip command\nat (HL) in bound bank.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nEntry: HL points into bound bank of Flash Memory sector of byte to be blown.\nExit: A = Chip Status Register; (bit 3 enabled indicates missing VPP pin); (bit 4 enabled\nindicates blow byte failure).\nChanges: AF.\nKeeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.","comment":"Byte Write Command"},
        "2945": {"comment":"to blow the byte at address..."},
        "2949": {"comment":"get back to Read Array Mode and return Status Register"},
        "2951": {"synopsis":"Wait for I28F0xxxx (INTEL) Flash Memory Chip command to finish.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nEntry: HL points into bound bank of potential Flash Memory.\nExit: A = Chip Status Register; Fz = 0, Command has been executed.\nChanges: AF.\nKeeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.","comment":"Read Status Register"},
        "2957": {"comment":"Chip still executing command..."},
        "2959": {"synopsis":"Polling code for AM29F0xxx / ST29F0xxx (AMD/STM) Flash Memory Chip ID\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nOZ source name: AM29Fx_PollChipId.\nEntry: HL = points into bound bank of potential Flash Memory (defined by OS_Fep sub\nfunction).\nExit: D = manufacturer code (at $00 xxx0 on chip); E = device code (at $00 xxx1 on chip).\nChanges: BC, DE, HL.\nKeeps: AF, IX, IY."},
        "2960": {"comment":"autoselect mode (to get ID)"},
        "2967": {"comment":"get Manufacturer Code (at XX00)"},
        "2969": {"comment":"get Device Code (at XX01)"},
        "2970": {"comment":"F0 -> (XXXXX), set Flash Memory to Read Array Mode"},
        "2972": {"comment":"D = Manufacturer Code, E = Device Code"},
        "2974": {"synopsis":"Erase block on an AMD 29Fxxxx (or compatible) Flash Memory, which is bound into segment x\nthat HL points into.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nOZ source name: AM29Fx_EraseSector.\nEntry: BC = $aa55; DE = address $x2AA (points into bound Flash Memory sector); HL = address\n$x555.\nExit: Success:; Fz = 1; A = undefined; Failure:; Fz = 0 (sector not erased).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.\nKeeps: IX, IY.","comment":"Execute main Erase Mode"},
        "2979": {"comment":"then sub command..."},
        "2984": {"synopsis":"Wait for AM29F0xxx (AMD) Flash Memory Chip command to finish.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nOZ source name: AM29Fx_ExeCommand.\nEntry: HL points into bound bank of potential Flash Memory.\nExit: A = undefined; Fz = 1, Command has been executed successfully; Fz = 0, Command\nexecution failed.\nChanges: AF, C.\nKeeps: B, DE, HL, IX, IY.","comment":"get first DQ6 programming status"},
        "2985": {"comment":"get a copy programming status (that is not XOR'ed)..."},
        "2986": {"comment":"get second DQ6 programming status"},
        "2987": {"comment":"toggling?"},
        "2989": {"comment":"no, command completed successfully (Read Array Mode active)!"},
        "2992": {"comment":"we're toggling with no error signal and waiting to complete..."},
        "2994": {"comment":"DQ5 went high, we need to get two successive status"},
        "2995": {"comment":"toggling reads to determine if we're still toggling"},
        "2996": {"comment":"which then indicates a command error..."},
        "2998": {"comment":"we're back in Read Array Mode, command completed successfully!"},
        "2999": {"comment":"command failed! F0 -> (XXXXX), force Flash Memory to Read Array Mode"},
        "3002": {"synopsis":"Blow byte in AM29F0xxx / ST29F0xxx (AMD/STM) Flash Memory Chip, identified by executing chip\ncommand at (HL) in bound bank.\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nEntry: A = byte to blow; BC = $aa55; DE = address $x2AA; HL = address $x555; hl' = points\ninto bound bank of Flash Memory sector to blow byte.\nExit: A = Chip Status Register.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.\nKeeps: IX, IY."},
        "3003": {"comment":"Byte Program Mode"},
        "3010": {"comment":"program byte to flash memory address"},
        "3013": {"synopsis":"Execute AM29F0xxx / ST29F0xxx (AMD/STM) Flash Memory Chip Command\n(Internal service routine in LOWRAM for OS_Fep system call)\nEntry: A = AMD/STM Command code; BC = $aa55; DE = address $x2AA; HL = address $x555.\nChanges: none.\nKeeps: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.","comment":"AA -> (X555), First Unlock Cycle"},
        "3015": {"comment":"55 -> (X2AA), Second Unlock Cycle"},
        "3017": {"comment":"A -> (X555), send command"},
        "3019": {"synopsis":"End of the LOWRAM code: the first address after the block that the reset code copies to RAM.\nThe assembly fails if it reaches SV_PRESERVED_RAM ($0400)."},
